cartridge "BB BARRAGE/Bill Chin/1988"

' BB BARRAGE
' A ColecoVision game based on "BB Barrage" by William Chin, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette August 1988 (Commodore 64 machine language, BB at $C000, with a
' BASIC screen editor). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: a light blue puck sits in the middle of the field. Each player
' has a BB gun in the middle of a goal line, one on the left, one on the
' right. Aim with the stick, hold the button for rapid fire: every BB that
' hits the puck pushes it a little. Knock the puck over your opponent's
' goal line. BBs bounce off the deflectors scattered over the field (the
' puck slides over them). After a minute without a goal the border turns
' red: destruct mode, and the BBs eat the deflectors away. Five goals win.
'
' From the original's machine code (orig/bb.dis): BBs fly 2 pixels a pass
' in 256 directions from the C64's own sine table ($C9AD); the guns turn
' one step a pass and fire every 5th pass while the button is held; 100
' BBs; a BB that leaves by a goal line goes to the gun on that side, one
' that hits the puck goes back to the pool; a gun with no BB may take one
' at random from the other side (the common pool, the default); the puck
' kick by where the BB strikes (the 10 x 8 table at $CA6B); the deflectors
' and their rules (round: a random direction; - and |: mirror; \ and /:
' the diagonal mirrors, $C0 - d and $40 - d); BBs and the puck bounce off
' the top and bottom; friction: on half the passes the puck loses 1/16 of
' its speed, and stops below 2; destruct mode after 3600 jiffies (one
' minute) without a goal, where a BB that hits a deflector takes that
' pixel with it; after a goal the BBs in flight go back to the pool, the
' puck returns to the middle, the deflectors are restored; 5 goals win
' (the editor offers 7, the game as shipped needs 5); the two fields: the
' program's own (at $CB02) and the disk's BB SCREEN.
'
' Liberties: the C64's 40 x 25 field is squeezed onto 28 x 21 cells (the
' deflectors' places rounded), speeds in ColecoVision pixels: one pass a
' frame (the C64 ran 1.2 passes a jiffy with 26 BBs flying, 0.5 with 86,
' 6 with none: measured in jsc64), the puck kicked by 7/8 of a BB's
' speed and at most 2 5/8 pixels a frame (the C64's clamp let a puck
' at full speed wrap round to full speed backwards: fixed); 48 BBs, not
' 100 (RAM, and the time a pass may take);
' the guns stop at straight up and straight down (the C64's wrapped round);
' the goal lines are where the puck's middle crosses the guns' line (the
' C64 counted the left side earlier than the right); a BB's dot is one
' pixel and hits the deflector shapes pixel for pixel; one player against
' the computer added (the C64 needed two), EASY or HARD on the title (see
' AiStick: it turns at a player's speed, sees the puck a quarter second
' late, leads it and pushes it off the player's line of fire, so holding
' the button without aiming loses); scores as numbers on a top line
' (the C64 drew crosses down the scorer's edge); pause on the right button
' (the C64 used f3); the editor is not ported (two fields to choose from);
' sounds and the title tune are new.
'
' Controls: left gun controller 1, right gun controller 2 (one player:
' either controller, against the computer's right gun). Stick up and down
' aims, left button fires (hold it for rapid fire), right button pauses.
' Title: up and down choose, a button chooses (the field and the
' computer's level toggle; HOW TO PLAY shows three pages).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128 on the
' title screens; the play field is a bitmap screen (as on the C64), its text
' drawn from a copy of the font; the frame loop is protected from the NMI;
' screens are drawn with the NMI off after a wait, display off. Music first
' in the sound table; the title tune plays once. `set sprite count` always.

